  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nSurface high pressure was centered southeast of the forecast \narea across southern IN/OH. Farther to the west, modest south- \nsouthwesterly low-level flow was in place from the Plains to the\nupper-Midwest and western Great Lakes region. This pattern is \nforecast to persist for much of the coming week, thanks to an \namplified upper-air pattern characterized by a ridge axis from \nTX-IL, downstream of a deep upper trough off the west coast \nwhich eventually shifts over the western CONUS. A mid-level wave\ncurrently transiting the base of the upper trough of the CA \ncoast is progged to lift northeast across the Rockies and the \nNorthern Plains Tuesday into Wednesday, with an associated \nsurface low. This will help to maintain our southwest low-level \nflow and warm advection, while low-level moisture return from \nthe western Gulf remains blocked by the western periphery of \nsurface ridging from eastern TX to the mid-Atlantic region. The\nsurface cold front associated with the aforementioned low is \nprogged to stall just north/northwest of our area Thursday, with\nonly some shallow low-level moisture return noted in forecast \nsoundings. Thus, the model trend toward dry conditions \npersisting through mid-week persists.\n\nWith the upper level ridge above or just off to the southeast \nof the area through late-week, temperatures are expected to \ncontinue to slowly warm. Highs by Thursday/Friday are forecast \nto be in the low-mid 60s, with overnight lows in the 40s. This \nis well-above our typical mid-40s/20s highs and lows for mid- \nlate November.\n\nLonger-range model/ensemble guidance is in general agreement in\nanother strong short wave and surface low tracking northeast \nfrom the Plains to the upper Midwest again in the Friday night \nthrough early Sunday period. This wave is currently progged to \nshift the long-wave trough farther to the east, providing better\nlow-level moisture return and supporting a stronger cold \nfrontal passage across the forecast area. This looks to be our \nnext chance for organize rainfall (with and ahead of the front \nSaturday/Saturday night), while the cold frontal passage early \nSunday would likely bring more seasonably cooler air to the \nregion.\n\nRatzer",
